# The installer medium's packages.
#
# This list is deliberately the smallest set that lets `oem-install` run: pacstrap and
# arch-chroot (arch-install-scripts), the partition and filesystem tools it calls, the network
# client it downloads Apple's images with, and enough diagnostics to say why a machine cannot
# host the guest. It is not a rescue toolkit.
#
# `tools/check-manifest.py` validates these names against the live Arch repositories in CI, the
# same way it validates host/packages/packages.txt, so a name that does not exist fails the
# build instead of producing a medium that fails at install time.

# --- base ----------------------------------------------------------------------
base
linux
linux-firmware
mkinitcpio
systemd
systemd-sysvcompat
pacman
archlinux-keyring

# --- the installer itself ------------------------------------------------------
# pacstrap, arch-chroot, genfstab.
arch-install-scripts
# parted, wipefs, blockdev, lsblk, udevadm.
util-linux
parted
# mkfs.fat and mkfs.ext4, which partition_disk calls.
dosfstools
e2fsprogs

# --- network -------------------------------------------------------------------
# check_network and the Apple download both need these.
iproute2
curl
ca-certificates
dnsmasq

# --- diagnostics ---------------------------------------------------------------
# check_gpu reads lspci; a user who hits a failure needs to see what was found.
pciutils
usbutils
hwdata
jq
less
nano
bash
coreutils
grep
sed
gawk
findutils
which

# --- bootloader for the installed system ---------------------------------------
# install_bootloader runs `bootctl` in the chroot; systemd provides it, and the ESP is written
# by it. Listed here as well because the medium's own systemd-boot needs it present.
efibootmgr
